  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 110 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nWidespread Rain Chances Sat Night - Mon:\n\nGlobal models depict the ridge breaking down on Saturday as the\naforementioned low shifts east. As it does so, the attendant\nsurface front will slog through the Plains and into northwestern\nMissouri. Destabilization and convergence will produce showers\nand thunderstorms along and ahead of the boundary. With the slow\nspeed of the front, rain chances will increase across the\nnorthwestern CWA late Saturday night and gradually translate to\nthe east-southeast throughout the day Sunday and finally move\nout on Monday. It should be stated, however, that all day \nwashouts are not expected as convection may strengthen and\ndiminish multiple times within this slow-moving system. \nOverall, the greatest rainfall totals are forecast north of I-44\nwhere the greatest PWATs and deepest moisture will be, but the \nhigher totals will likely be quite localized. \n\nNBM Probabilistic Rainfall (Sat Night thru Mon):\n>0.10 inch: 50-70%\n>0.25 inch: 40-60%\n>1.00 inch: 10-25%\n\nCooler Temperatures Early Next Week:\n\nYes, the cold front will indeed bring cooler temperatures to the\nregion early next week. With the continued slower trend of the\nfrontal passage on Sunday, it remains possible that an extension\nof the Heat Advisory will be needed across southern Missouri for\nan additional day, but questions on timing, temperatures, dew \npoints, and convective coverage limit confidence too greatly to\nmake that determination with this forecast package. \n\nAfter Sunday, confidence is high that temperatures will\ndecrease. There remains a moderate amount of uncertainty in\nexactly how much cooler they will be, however, with ensemble\nclusters continuing to show disagreement in just how much the\nridge gets dampened. That said, the NBM interquartile range\nwould support near-average high temperatures for much of the\narea on Monday (near 80 deg) and even *below-average* \ntemperatures by Tuesday. Again, details like frontal timing,\ncloud cover, and convective coverage will ultimately drive the\ntemperatures any one location will experience, but the signal\nfor comparatively cooler temperatures is clear.",
